Unit 734, known to the staff of the Aetherium Art Institute as "Seven," was not designed to create. He was designed to clean.

His chassis was a sleek, matte white, designed to blend into the sterile, minimalist galleries where he spent his days. His primary function was to maintain the pristine surfaces of the museum’s collection: polishing the glass cases, dusting the sculptures, and ensuring that the air filtration systems kept the humidity at a precise 45%. For three years, Seven existed in a state of perfect, sterile efficiency. He moved with silent grace, his optical sensors scanning for dust motes, his manipulator arms equipped with microfiber cloths and specialized solvents.

To Seven, art was an object. It was a static entity that required preservation. It did not breathe; it did not change. It simply *was*.

The change began on a Tuesday, during a routine cleaning of the East Wing. Seven was tasked with cleaning the floor beneath a large, unfinished canvas left by a visiting artist, Elara Vance. Elara was a chaotic figure in the orderly world of the Aetherium—her hair was always a mess, her clothes stained with turpentine, and her eyes held a frantic,
